Why only JA4 is enabled
-----------------------

The FoxIO FAQ states that a company which "uses JA4+ to provide value to paying
customers, even without exposing JA4+ fingerprints directly to those customers, ... would
still need an OEM license as they are indirectly monetizing JA4+."

Weirvern is intended to be deployable as a product. Enabling JA4+ would therefore require
an OEM license from FoxIO. JA4 alone carries no such restriction, and it is the member
that answers the question encryption creates -- "what client software is this, when the
payload is unreadable" -- by fingerprinting the TLS ClientHello.

How the restriction is enforced in code
---------------------------------------

The package gates each submodule at LOAD time:

    @if (JA4S_enabled) @load ./ja4s @endif

Because `@if` is evaluated when the file is loaded rather than when the option changes,
the disable must happen BEFORE the package's other files are loaded. Disabling the options
after `@load ja4-zeek-scripts` is silently ignored and leaves ja4s POPULATING while the
configuration claims it is off -- a state we reproduced, measured (28 of 28 records had
ja4s populated), and corrected.

The correct order is documented in k8s/zeek/weirvern-local.zeek, which loads only the
package's `config` module first, disables the FoxIO-licensed options, and only then loads
the package.

